% SQL conformance cases for the ORD_SUFFIX mapping. These go in the matching
% sql/cases/*.sqlt and pin the string every host emits -- which is what makes
% "all five hosts translate identically" a check rather than a hope.
%
% What they do NOT establish is that the database agrees with SEL about what the
% answer means. Only sql/oracle/ does that, run against real servers by
% tools/oracle-db.sh.
%
% EXAMPLE-BEGIN
### name: funcs.ord-suffix.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "table": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
ORD_SUFFIX(N)
--- expect
CONCAT(`t`.`n`, CASE WHEN MOD(`t`.`n`, 100) BETWEEN 11 AND 13 THEN 'th' WHEN MOD(`t`.`n`, 10) = 1 THEN 'st' WHEN MOD(`t`.`n`, 10) = 2 THEN 'nd' WHEN MOD(`t`.`n`, 10) = 3 THEN 'rd' ELSE 'th' END)
===
### name: funcs.ord-suffix.unmapped-is-refused
--- note
sqlite has no entry, so a rule using it is refused rather than emitted against a
function the server does not have. This case is the one that would fail if a
future edit mapped it carelessly on a base instead of per dialect.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "table": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
ORD_SUFFIX(N)
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
% EXAMPLE-END
